##
   The permission a `mut` grants is per *dereference depth*, which is why a
   `mut` on a pointee does not let the pointer itself be reassigned.

   `var p: ptr<mut i32>` has the mutable type one dereference in. So `^p = 5`
   is legal -- the write goes through the pointee, at depth 1 -- while `p = q`
   is not: that would be reassigning the binding, at depth 0, and the binding's
   own type is `ptr<...>`, not `mut`.

   The corollary is that `mut` belongs on the pointee. Dereferencing does not
   strip a `mut` qualifier, so `mut ptr<T>` is not dereferenceable at all and
   would be the wrong spelling.
##
func through_pointee(): i32 {
  var x: mut i32 = 1i32;
  var p: ptr<mut i32> = @x;
  var v: i32 = marked { ^p = 5i32; 0i32 };
  return v;
}

func read_immutable_pointee(): i32 {
  var x: i32 = 1i32;
  var p: ptr<i32> = @x;
  var v: i32 = marked { ^p; 0i32 };
  return v;
}
